SYSTEM OVERVIEW
===============
The system is a Role-Based Access Control (RBAC) system in a multi-user environment, supporting role hierarchy, temporary delegation of roles, revocation of delegations, and concurrent request processing preserving authorization correctness, delegation and permission consistency, and system liveness and safety. Users hold security clearance levels (High, Medium, Low) and assigned or delegated roles subject to clearance constraints. Requests affecting classified objects are submitted and processed concurrently, with FIFO order under Normal mode. Higher-clearance users may approve or reject lower-clearance requests and delegate roles temporarily, respecting clearance requirements. Delegations expire after fixed time or may be revoked.

The Emergency Module introduces an Emergency mode triggered by exactly two administrators concurrently during Normal mode, lasting for a limited duration before returning to Normal mode. Emergency mode modifies request processing order, role exclusivity, and administrative privileges. Upon exit, emergency privileges and delegated roles during Emergency are revoked, and administrators update credentials.

EXISTING-SYSTEM ASSUMPTIONS AND CONSTRAINTS
============================================
- Users may submit requests only if request classification ≤ user's clearance.
- Higher-clearance users may approve/reject lower-clearance requests.
- Delegated roles require delegate to meet role’s minimum clearance.
- Delegated roles expire after fixed time or may be revoked by administrators.
- Request processing is concurrent and ensures every request is eventually processed.
- Under Normal mode, request processing strictly follows FIFO order.

PROSPECTIVE FUNCTIONAL REQUIREMENTS
====================================
R1: Emergency Mode Triggering  
Exactly two administrators must trigger Emergency mode concurrently during Normal mode.  
*Concurrency:* "Concurrently" means within a bounded discrete integer time window of 1 discrete time unit (parameterizable).  
*Note:* The concurrency window size and semantics are explicitly defined to avoid ambiguity.

R2: Emergency Mode Duration and Termination  
Emergency mode lasts a limited duration defined as a fixed numeric bound of 10 discrete integer time steps (parameterizable). After this strict upper bound, system returns to Normal mode.

R3: Behavior During Emergency Mode  
R3.1: Request Processing Priority  
FIFO order may be bypassed to prioritize simultaneous processing of multiple High-classification requests.  
R3.2: Temporary Mutual Exclusivity Suspension  
Mutual exclusivity constraints on a specified explicit subset or according to clearly defined criteria of roles are suspended during Emergency mode, allowing simultaneous holding of normally exclusive roles.  
R3.3: Emergency Privileges for Administrators  
Administrators receive emergency privileges during Emergency mode, which are distinct from normal privileges; these privileges are either explicitly enumerated or abstracted as a defined set of emergency-enabled administrators.

R4: Post-Emergency Mode Actions  
R4.1: Revocation of Emergency Privileges  
Emergency privileges are revoked immediately after Emergency mode ends.  
R4.2: Revocation of Delegated Roles During Emergency  
Delegated roles granted during Emergency mode are revoked upon Emergency mode exit.  
R4.3: Credential Update by Administrators  
Administrators must update credentials after Emergency mode ends, modeled as explicit event occurrences (e.g., Boolean flags), reflecting audit/logging requirements.

KEY CONCEPTS AND ENTITIES
==========================
- User: Entity with security clearance holding assigned/delegated roles, submitting requests.  
- Security Clearance Level: High, Medium, Low; governs role assignment and request submission.  
- Administrator: User with High clearance and administrative privileges, including Emergency triggering and delegation revocation.  
- Role: Permission set assigned to users; roles have minimum clearance requirements.  
- Delegated Role: Temporarily granted role by higher-clearance user, subject to clearance and revocation.  
- Request: User-submitted action affecting system objects, classified by highest classification level of affected objects.  
- System Object: Entity affected by requests, classified High, Medium, Low.  
- System Mode: Normal or Emergency.  
- Emergency Privileges: Temporary privileges granted exclusively during Emergency mode, distinct from normal privileges.  
- Mutual Exclusive Roles: Roles that cannot be simultaneously held under normal conditions; a specified explicit subset suspends exclusivity during Emergency mode.  
- FIFO Processing Order: Strict request order under Normal mode.  
- Time: Discrete integer steps enabling temporal arithmetic and ordering, modeled via total injective function timeValue: Time -> Int.

ASSUMPTIONS REQUIRING CLARIFICATION
====================================
1. Concurrency window size for Emergency triggering (currently 1 discrete time unit, parameterizable).  
2. Numeric bound for Emergency mode duration (currently fixed at 10 discrete time units, parameterizable).  
3. Explicit subset or criteria for mutual exclusivity constraints or roles suspended during Emergency mode.  
4. Definition or abstraction level of emergency privileges (explicit enumeration vs. abstract set).  
5. Modeling of credential updates as explicit event occurrences (e.g., Boolean flags) post-Emergency, aligned with audit/logging requirements.  
6. Whether request classification clearance enforcement remains unchanged during Emergency mode or if exceptions apply.  
7. Whether modeling multiple or overlapping Emergency mode occurrences is required or single-instance suffices.

CONSTRAINTS
===========
- Users submit requests only if request classification ≤ user's clearance.  
- Higher-clearance users may approve/reject lower-clearance requests.  
- Delegated roles require delegate meets role’s minimum clearance.  
- Delegated roles expire after fixed time or may be revoked.  
- Request processing is concurrent and ensures liveness.  
- Under Normal mode, request processing follows strict FIFO.  
- Emergency mode triggered only by exactly two administrators within concurrency window during Normal mode.  
- Emergency mode duration strictly bounded by fixed discrete integer time steps, then returns to Normal mode.  
- During Emergency mode, FIFO order may be bypassed to prioritize simultaneous High-classification requests.  
- Mutual exclusivity constraints on specified explicit subset of roles suspended during Emergency mode.  
- Emergency privileges granted only during Emergency mode and revoked immediately after.  
- Delegated roles granted during Emergency revoked upon exit.  
- Administrators perform credential update as explicit event after Emergency mode end.  
- Request classification clearance enforcement remains unchanged during Emergency mode (subject to confirmation).  
- Time modeled as discrete integer steps enabling temporal arithmetic and ordering.
