SYSTEM OVERVIEW  
===============  
The system is a Role-Based Access Control (RBAC) system in a multi-user environment supporting role hierarchy, temporary delegation, revocation, and concurrent request processing preserving authorization correctness, delegation consistency, and system safety. Users hold security clearance levels and assigned/delegated roles subject to clearance constraints. Requests affecting classified objects are submitted and processed concurrently, with FIFO order under Normal mode. Higher-clearance users may approve/reject requests and delegate roles temporarily, respecting clearance requirements. Delegations expire or may be revoked.

The Emergency Module introduces an Emergency mode triggered by exactly two administrators concurrently during Normal mode, lasting for a limited duration before returning to Normal mode. Emergency mode modifies request processing, role exclusivity, and administrative privileges. Upon exit, emergency privileges and delegated roles granted during Emergency are revoked, and administrators update credentials.

EXISTING-SYSTEM ASSUMPTIONS AND CONSTRAINTS  
============================================  
- Users submit requests only if request classification ≤ user's clearance.  
- Higher-clearance users may approve/reject lower-clearance requests.  
- Delegated roles require delegate to meet role’s minimum clearance.  
- Delegated roles expire after fixed time or may be revoked.  
- Request processing is concurrent and ensures liveness.  
- Under Normal mode, request processing strictly follows FIFO order.  

PROSPECTIVE FUNCTIONAL REQUIREMENTS  
====================================  
R1: Emergency Mode Triggering  
Exactly two administrators must trigger Emergency mode concurrently during Normal mode.  
*Concurrency Definition:* "Concurrently" means within a bounded discrete integer time window of 1 discrete time unit by default, parameterizable for flexibility and future refinement.

R2: Emergency Mode Duration and Termination  
Emergency mode lasts a limited duration defined as a fixed numeric bound of 10 discrete integer time steps by default, parameterizable. Upon reaching this strict upper bound, the system returns to Normal mode.

R3: Behavior During Emergency Mode  
R3.1: Request Processing Priority  
During Emergency mode, FIFO order may be bypassed to prioritize simultaneous processing of multiple High-classification requests, ensuring timely handling of critical operations.  
R3.2: Temporary Mutual Exclusivity Suspension  
Mutual exclusivity constraints on an explicit subset of roles — clearly defined by system criteria or enumerated lists — are suspended during Emergency mode, enabling flexible administrative actions.  
R3.3: Emergency Privileges for Administrators  
Administrators receive emergency privileges during Emergency mode, distinct from normal privileges. These privileges are explicitly enumerated or abstracted as a defined set.

R4: Post-Emergency Mode Actions  
R4.1: Revocation of Emergency Privileges  
Emergency privileges revoked immediately after Emergency mode ends.  
R4.2: Revocation of Delegated Roles During Emergency  
Delegated roles granted during Emergency mode revoked upon Emergency exit.  
R4.3: Credential Update by Administrators  
Administrators update credentials after Emergency mode ends, modeled explicitly as event occurrences temporally correlated with Emergency mode termination, reflecting audit/logging requirements.

