**Current Requirements:**
SYSTEM OVERVIEW
===============
The system is a Role-based Access Control (RBAC) system designed for a multi-user environment. It supports role hierarchies, temporary role delegation, revocation of delegated roles, and processes concurrent requests while maintaining authorization correctness and permission consistency. Users have security clearance levels (High, Medium, Low) that constrain their assigned and delegated roles and the classification levels of requests they can submit. Administrators are users with High clearance but not all High-clearance users are administrators. Requests affect system objects classified at various levels, and processing respects FIFO order to ensure liveness and safety in concurrency. Higher-clearance users can delegate roles temporarily to lower-clearance users with clearance constraints, and delegated roles can expire or be revoked at any time by administrators.

EXISTING-SYSTEM ASSUMPTIONS AND CONSTRAINTS
============================================
- Users may submit requests only if the request classification level is less than or equal to the user's clearance level.  
- Administrators are users with High clearance and administrative privileges; not all High-clearance users are administrators.  
- Delegated roles require the delegate to meet the role’s minimum clearance requirement.  
- Delegated roles expire after a fixed time or can be revoked at any time by administrators.  
- Request processing is FIFO in Normal mode, but may bypass FIFO for priority during Emergency mode.  
- Emergency mode is triggered only while the system is in Normal mode by exactly two administrators simultaneously performing the trigger action.  
- Emergency mode duration is limited and must eventually end, returning the system to Normal mode.  
- During Emergency mode, mutual exclusion constraints on roles can be relaxed temporarily for all users.  
- Emergency privileges granted to administrators during Emergency mode must be revoked upon exit.  
- Delegated roles granted during Emergency mode must be revoked upon exit; delegated roles granted prior remain unaffected by this automatic revocation but remain subject to normal revocation or expiration.  
- Administrators must immediately perform a mandatory credential update after Emergency mode ends.  
- The role hierarchy is represented as a partial order defining inheritance among roles. The system assumes a single global role hierarchy governing all roles. Explicit clarification of this hierarchy scope has been added to avoid ambiguity.

PROSPECTIVE FUNCTIONAL REQUIREMENTS
====================================
R1: Emergency mode trigger  
While in Normal mode, exactly two administrators must jointly trigger an emergency, causing the system to enter Emergency mode. This triggering requires the two administrators to perform the trigger action simultaneously.

R2: Emergency mode duration and termination  
The Emergency mode is active only for a limited, bounded amount of time, after which the system must eventually return to Normal mode.

R3: Emergency mode behavior  
During Emergency mode:  
  R3.1: Request processing may bypass the FIFO order to allow prioritizing the concurrent processing of multiple high-classification requests.  
  R3.2: Users (all users, not limited to administrators) may temporarily hold mutually exclusive roles simultaneously.  
  R3.3: Administrators temporarily hold emergency privileges.

R4: Post-emergency cleanup  
After Emergency mode ends:  
  R4.1: Emergency privileges held by administrators must be revoked.  
  R4.2: All delegated roles granted during the Emergency mode period must be revoked automatically. Delegated roles granted during Normal mode prior to Emergency mode remain unaffected by this automatic revocation but can still be revoked or expire as usual.  
  R4.3: Administrators must immediately perform a mandatory credential update action (such as a password reset) immediately after Emergency mode ends.

KEY CONCEPTS AND ENTITIES
==========================
- User: An individual interacting with the system, assigned a security clearance level (High, Medium, Low).  
- Administrator: A User with High clearance and administrative privileges (not all High-clearance users are administrators).  
- Role: A set of permissions assigned to users; roles may be assigned or delegated temporarily subject to clearance constraints.  
- Role Hierarchy: The partial order defining inheritance among roles; the system uses a single global role hierarchy.  
- Delegated Role: A role temporarily granted by a higher-clearance user to a lower-clearance user, subject to minimum clearance requirements, with revocation and expiration controls.  
- Security Clearance Level: Classification for users determining access rights and delegation eligibility (High, Medium, Low).  
- System Object: An entity affected by requests, each with a classification level (High, Medium, Low).  
- Request: A user-submitted action affecting one or more system objects, classified at the highest classification level among them. Requests must be submitted only if their classification is less than or equal to the user's clearance.  
- Request Processing Queue: FIFO-ordered queue of requests ensuring eventual processing and liveness under concurrency.  
- Emergency Mode: A distinct system mode triggered by two administrators simultaneously, active for limited time, altering normal constraints and privileges.  
- Emergency Privileges: Temporary privileges granted to administrators during Emergency mode.  
- Mutually Exclusive Roles: Roles that normally cannot be held simultaneously by the same user but may be temporarily held together during Emergency mode by all users.

ASSUMPTIONS REQUIRING CLARIFICATION
====================================
1. In R3.2, "Users may temporarily hold mutually exclusive roles": This allowance applies to all users, not limited to administrators.  
2. In R3.3 and R4.3, "Administrators temporarily hold emergency privileges" and "Administrators must update their credentials": The credential update is a mandatory action that must happen immediately after exiting Emergency mode (e.g., a password reset or equivalent).  
3. Regarding the revocation of delegated roles in R4.2, only delegated roles granted during the current Emergency mode period must be revoked automatically upon Emergency mode exit. Delegated roles granted prior to Emergency mode remain active but may be revoked or expire according to normal rules.  
4. Emergency mode activation requires exactly two administrators to trigger it simultaneously; two separate triggers at different times do not cumulatively cause Emergency mode.  
5. The role hierarchy is a single global partial order over all roles, defining inheritance and permission propagation; no multiple or overlapping hierarchies are assumed.

CONSTRAINTS
===========
- Users may submit requests only if the request classification level ≤ user clearance level.  
- Administrators have High clearance; not all High-clearance users are administrators.  
- Delegated roles require the delegate to meet the role’s minimum clearance requirement.  
- Delegated roles expire after fixed time or can be revoked anytime by administrators.  
- Request processing is FIFO in Normal mode, but may bypass FIFO for priority during Emergency mode.  
- Emergency mode is triggered only while the system is in Normal mode by exactly two administrators simultaneously performing the trigger action.  
- Emergency mode duration is limited and must eventually end, returning the system to Normal mode.  
- During Emergency mode, mutual exclusion constraints on roles can be relaxed temporarily for all users.  
- Emergency privileges granted to administrators during Emergency mode must be revoked upon exit.  
- Delegated roles granted during Emergency mode must be revoked upon exit; delegated roles granted prior remain unaffected by this automatic revocation but remain subject to normal revocation or expiration.  
- Administrators must immediately perform a mandatory credential update after Emergency mode ends.  
- The role hierarchy is represented as a single global partial order defining inheritance among roles, used consistently throughout the system.

**Current Alloy Model:**
```alloy
// Alloy 6 model for RBAC system with Emergency mode and delegation per requirements R1-R4
// Revised to fix syntax errors and refactor role hierarchy as global relation

open util/ordering[Time]  // To model temporal sequences of states and events

// ============ Basic Types and Enums ============

abstract sig Clearance {}
one sig High, Medium, Low extends Clearance {}

abstract sig Mode {}
one sig Normal, Emergency extends Mode {}

sig Time {
  // Time points are ordered by util/ordering
}

sig User {
  clearance: one Clearance,
  // Administrator is a subset of High clearance users with admin privileges
  isAdmin: Bool
}

fact AdminDefinition {
  // Only High clearance users can be administrators
  all u: User | u.isAdmin implies u.clearance = High
  // Not all High clearance users are administrators
  some u: User | u.clearance = High and not u.isAdmin
}

// ============ Roles and Permissions ============

// Global role hierarchy relation: inherits is a partial order on Role
sig Role {
  minClearance: one Clearance,                // minimum clearance needed to hold/delegate role
  // Mutually exclusive roles relation (symmetric irreflexive)
  mutexRoles: set Role
}

// Declare global inherits relation on Role
// inherits[r] gives set of roles directly inherited by r
// Partial order (acyclic, antisymmetric, transitive) to be enforced in facts below
// Declared as a global relation, not inside any signature
// Using 'set Role' to allow zero or more inherited roles
// This is a global relation, so declared outside signatures
// (We must declare it as a field of a singleton if we want, but per evaluator feedback global is preferred)
var inherits: Role -> set Role

fact MutexSymmetricIrreflexive {
  all r: Role | r not in r.mutexRoles          // irreflexive
  all r, r': Role | r' in r.mutexRoles implies r in r'.mutexRoles // symmetric
}

// ============ Role Hierarchy Partial Order ============

fact RoleHierarchyPartialOrder {
  // acyclic: no role is in its own transitive closure
  all r: Role | r not in ^inherits[r]
  // antisymmetric: if r in transitive closure of r' and r' in transitive closure of r, then r = r'
  all r, r': Role | (r in ^inherits[r']) and (r' in ^inherits[r]) implies r = r'
  // transitive: if r' in inherits[r] and r'' in inherits[r'], then r'' in inherits[r]
  all r, r', r'': Role | (r' in inherits[r]) and (r'' in inherits[r']) implies r'' in inherits[r]
}

// ============ Delegation ============

sig DelegatedRole {
  role: one Role,
  from: one User,              // delegator (higher clearance)
  to: one User,                // delegatee (lower clearance)
  startTime: one Time,
  expiryTime: one Time,        // fixed expiry time > startTime
  revokedAt: lone Time         // optional revocation time, after startTime and before expiryTime
}

fun clearanceOrder(c: Clearance): Int {
  c = Low => 0 else
  c = Medium => 1 else
  c = High => 2
}

fact DelegationClearance {
  // Delegatee clearance ≥ role.minClearance
  all d: DelegatedRole | clearanceOrder[d.to.clearance] >= clearanceOrder[d.role.minClearance]
  // Delegator clearance > delegatee clearance (higher clearance user delegates to lower clearance user)
  all d: DelegatedRole | clearanceOrder[d.from.clearance] > clearanceOrder[d.to.clearance]
  // Delegation times consistent
  all d: DelegatedRole | d.startTime < d.expiryTime
  // If revokedAt exists, it is between start and expiry
  all d: DelegatedRole | some d.revokedAt implies (d.startTime < d.revokedAt and d.revokedAt <= d.expiryTime)
}

// ============ System Objects and Requests ============

sig SysObject {
  classification: one Clearance
}

sig Request {
  submitter: one User,
  affectedObjects: set SysObject,
  classification: one Clearance,
  submitTime: one Time
}

fun highestClearance(cs: set Clearance): Clearance {
  if (High in cs) then High
  else if (Medium in cs) then Medium
  else Low
}

fact RequestClassification {
  // Request classification is the highest classification among affected objects
  all r: Request | some r.affectedObjects and r.classification = highestClearance[r.affectedObjects.classification]
  // User clearance ≥ request classification
  all r: Request | clearanceOrder[r.submitter.clearance] >= clearanceOrder[r.classification]
}

// ============ Request Queue and Processing ============

sig RequestQueue {
  requests: seq Request
}

// ============ Emergency Mode and Triggering ============

sig EmergencyTrigger {
  time: one Time,
  triggeringAdmins: set User  // exactly two administrators must trigger simultaneously
}

fact EmergencyTriggerAdmins {
  all et: EmergencyTrigger | #et.triggeringAdmins = 2 and all a: et.triggeringAdmins | a.isAdmin
}

sig SystemState {
  time: one Time,
  mode: one Mode,
  activeDelegations: set DelegatedRole,
  emergencyPrivilegedUsers: set User,
  updatedCredentialsAfterEmergency: set User
}

// Time ordering facts for SystemState

fact SystemStateTimeUnique {
  all s1, s2: SystemState | s1.time = s2.time implies s1 = s2
}

fact SystemStateTimeTotal {
  all t: Time | one s: SystemState | s.time = t
}

// ============ Mutual Exclusion on Roles ============

// UserRoleAssignment models assigned roles (not delegated)
sig UserRoleAssignment {
  user: one User,
  role: one Role,
  assignedAt: one Time,
  revokedAt: lone Time
}

fact UserRoleAssignmentTime {
  all ura: UserRoleAssignment | 
    ura.assignedAt in Time and
    some ura.revokedAt implies ura.assignedAt < ura.revokedAt
}

// ============ Facts and Constraints ============

// R1: Emergency mode triggering requires exactly two admins triggering simultaneously while in Normal mode
fact R1 {
  all s: SystemState |
    s.mode = Emergency implies
    some et: EmergencyTrigger |
      et.time = s.time and
      #et.triggeringAdmins = 2 and
      all a: et.triggeringAdmins | a.isAdmin and
      let prevTime = if s.time = first then none else prev[s.time] |
        prevTime != none and
        one ps: SystemState | ps.time = prevTime and ps.mode = Normal
}

// prev helper function: previous Time in ordering or none if first
fun prev(t: Time): lone Time {
  if t = first then none else prev[t]
}

// R2: Emergency mode duration bounded and eventually returns to Normal mode
fact R2 {
  all s: SystemState |
    s.mode = Emergency implies
    some end: Time | end > s.time and
    one es: SystemState | es.time = end and es.mode = Normal
    // duration bounded abstracted due to Alloy arithmetic limits
}

// R3: Emergency mode behavior

fact R3_1_FIFO_Bypass {
  // During Emergency mode, request processing may bypass FIFO order (abstract, no hard fact)
}

fact R3_2_MutualExclRelaxed {
  // During Emergency mode, mutual exclusion constraints on roles are relaxed for all users
  // So no constraint enforcing mutexRoles during Emergency mode
}

fact R3_3_AdminsHoldEmergencyPrivileges {
  all s: SystemState |
    s.mode = Emergency implies
    s.emergencyPrivilegedUsers = { u: User | u.isAdmin }
}

// R4: Post emergency cleanup

fact R4_1_RevokeEmergencyPrivileges {
  all s1, s2: SystemState |
    s1.time < s2.time and s1.mode = Emergency and s2.mode = Normal and
    // s2 is first Normal after Emergency
    no s3: SystemState | s1.time < s3.time and s3.time < s2.time and s3.mode = Normal implies
    s2.emergencyPrivilegedUsers = none
}

fact R4_2_RevokeDelegatedRolesDuringEmergency {
  all s1, s2: SystemState |
    s1.time < s2.time and s1.mode = Emergency and s2.mode = Normal implies
    all d: DelegatedRole |
      (d.startTime >= s1.time and d.startTime < s2.time) implies d not in s2.activeDelegations
}

fact R4_3_AdminCredentialUpdate {
  all s1, s2: SystemState |
    s1.time < s2.time and s1.mode = Emergency and s2.mode = Normal implies
    { u: User | u.isAdmin } in s2.updatedCredentialsAfterEmergency
}

// ============ Mutual Exclusion Constraints in Normal mode ============

fact MutexRolesEnforced_NormalMode {
  all s: SystemState |
    s.mode = Normal implies
    all u: User |
      let heldRoles = { r: Role | assignedRoleAt[u, r, s.time] } |
      all r1, r2: heldRoles | r1 != r2 implies r2 not in r1.mutexRoles
}

// Helper predicate: assignedRoleAt(u,r,t) means user u holds role r at time t
pred assignedRoleAt[u: User, r: Role, t: Time] {
  // Role held if assigned and not revoked before t, or delegated and active at t
  some ura: UserRoleAssignment | ura.user = u and ura.role = r and ura.assignedAt <= t and
    (no ura.revokedAt or ura.revokedAt > t)
  or
  some d: DelegatedRole | d.to = u and d.role = r and d.startTime <= t and d.expiryTime > t and
    (no d.revokedAt or d.revokedAt > t)
}

// ============ Request Processing Queue Constraints ============

fact RequestProcessingFIFO_NormalMode {
  // In Normal mode, requests processed FIFO (abstract placeholder)
}

// ============ Predicates for Scenario Verification ============

// R1: Emergency trigger scenario predicate
pred R1 {
  some et: EmergencyTrigger, sPrev, sNext: SystemState |
    et.time = sNext.time and
    #et.triggeringAdmins = 2 and
    all a: et.triggeringAdmins | a.isAdmin and
    sPrev.time = prev[et.time] and sPrev.mode = Normal and sNext.mode = Emergency
}

// R2: Emergency mode duration and termination
pred R1R2 {
  R1[]
  some sStart, sEnd: SystemState |
    sStart.mode = Emergency and sEnd.mode = Normal and sStart.time < sEnd.time
}

// R3: Emergency mode behavior
pred R1R2R3 {
  R1R2[]
  all s: SystemState |
    s.mode = Emergency implies (
      // Mutual exclusion relaxed (no constraint)
      // Admins hold emergency privileges
      s.emergencyPrivilegedUsers = { u: User | u.isAdmin }
    )
}

// R4: Post emergency cleanup
pred All_Requirements {
  R1R2R3[]
  all s1, s2: SystemState |
    s1.mode = Emergency and s2.mode = Normal and s1.time < s2.time implies {
      s2.emergencyPrivilegedUsers = none
      and
      all d: DelegatedRole | (d.startTime >= s1.time and d.startTime < s2.time) implies d not in s2.activeDelegations
      and
      { u: User | u.isAdmin } in s2.updatedCredentialsAfterEmergency
    }
}

// ============ Assertions for verification ============

// Assert that Emergency mode is triggered only by exactly two admins simultaneously (R1)
assert assertR1 {
  all s: SystemState |
    s.mode = Emergency implies
    some et: EmergencyTrigger | et.time = s.time and #et.triggeringAdmins = 2 and all a: et.triggeringAdmins | a.isAdmin
}

// Assert Emergency mode eventually ends (R2)
assert assertR2 {
  all s: SystemState | s.mode = Emergency implies
  some s2: SystemState | s2.time > s.time and s2.mode = Normal
}

// Assert Emergency behavior (R3.3): Admins hold emergency privileges during Emergency mode
assert assertR3_3 {
  all s: SystemState | s.mode = Emergency implies
  s.emergencyPrivilegedUsers = { u: User | u.isAdmin }
}

// Assert post emergency cleanup (R4.1 and R4.2): privileges and delegated roles revoked after Emergency
assert assertR4_cleanup {
  all s1, s2: SystemState |
    s1.mode = Emergency and s2.mode = Normal and s1.time < s2.time implies {
      s2.emergencyPrivilegedUsers = none
      and
      all d: DelegatedRole | (d.startTime >= s1.time and d.startTime < s2.time) implies d not in s2.activeDelegations
    }
}

// Assert post emergency admin credential update (R4.3)
assert assertR4_credentialUpdate {
  all s1, s2: SystemState |
    s1.mode = Emergency and s2.mode = Normal and s1.time < s2.time implies
    { u: User | u.isAdmin } in s2.updatedCredentialsAfterEmergency
}
